186562595 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536056 17592186045443 true] [17592186562595 1071 "Given that $f(r,\\theta)=- r^{2} (\\cos {4 \\theta} - 2)$, find $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$, where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." 13194139536056 17592186045443 true] [17592186562595 1072 #uuid "0480635c-deef-558b-bf50-7e4a5cabfaac" 13194139536056 17592186045443 true] [17592186562595 1074 "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂r}\\cos \\theta-\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta}\\sin \\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂r}=- 2 r (\\cos {4 \\theta} - 2),\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta}=4 r^{2} \\sin {4 \\theta}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =(- 2 r (\\cos {4 \\theta} - 2))\\cos \\theta-\\frac{1}{r}(4 r^{2} \\sin {4 \\theta})\\sin \\theta \\\\ & =4r\\cos \\theta-2r\\cos 4\\theta\\cos \\theta-4r\\sin 4\\theta\\sin \\theta \\\\ & =-r(-4\\cos \\theta+2\\cos 4\\theta\\cos \\theta+4\\sin 4\\theta\\sin \\theta).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536056 17592186045443 true] [17592186562596 1080 "9 r^{2} (\\cos {4 \\theta} - 2) \\cos {\\theta} - 12 r^{2} \\sin {\\theta} \\sin {4 \\theta}" 13194139536056 17592186045443 true] [17592186562596 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139536056 17592186045443 true] [17592186562596 1083 #uuid "06c911de-2d6d-5671-a74c-00fc4aa3eaa0" 13194139536056 17592186045443 true] [17592186562597 1068 #edb/ref 17592186562625 13194139536056 17592186045443 true] [17592186562597 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536056 17592186045443 true] [17592186562597 1071 "Given that $u(r,\\theta)=3 r^{3} (\\cos {4 \\theta} - 2)$, find $\\frac{∂u}{∂x}$, where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." 13194139536056 17592186045443 true] [1759218